POSTS={
"air-mover-vs-dehumidifier":{
"title":"Air Mover or Dehumidifier: What Each One Does",
"desc":"Air movers evaporate water from materials. Dehumidifiers remove it from the air. Using one without the other leaves a house wet.",
"h1":"Air Mover or Dehumidifier: What Each One Does",
"eyebrow":"Drying science","img":"drying-equipment-staged-largo-garage.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Air movers and dehumidifiers do opposite halves of the same job, and in a Largo water damage job you need both. An air mover drives moisture out of wet material into the air. A dehumidifier pulls that moisture out of the air before it settles somewhere else in the house.",
 "Run one without the other and you are not drying the building. You are either moving wet air around or waiting for material to release moisture that nothing is carrying away."],
"sections":[
 ("What Each Machine Actually Does",[
  ("table",["","Air mover","Dehumidifier"],
   [["Job","Speeds evaporation from wet surfaces","Removes water vapour from the air"],
    ["Acts on","Materials: carpet, drywall, subfloor","Room air"],
    ["Without it","Material holds moisture even in dry air","Humidity climbs and moisture lands in cool cavities"],
    ["Placement","Angled along surfaces, not pointed straight on","Central, with clear intake and discharge"]])]),
 ("Why an Air Mover Is Not a Fan",[
  "A household fan moves air around a room. An air mover is built to push a high velocity sheet of air along a surface, which strips away the thin layer of saturated air sitting against wet material.",
  "That boundary layer is what slows evaporation. Break it and the material gives up moisture quickly. Leave it and the surface stays damp regardless of how much air is moving elsewhere in the room."]),
 ("Why Florida Makes the Dehumidifier the Critical Half",[
  "Pinellas County dew points sit in the seventies for much of the year. Air that is already near saturation cannot accept much more moisture, so evaporation slows exactly when you need it fastest.",
  "Running air movers alone in that climate raises indoor humidity and pushes moisture into cool wall cavities and closets. That is how a drying attempt turns into a mold job."]),
 ("Low Grain Refrigerant and Why It Matters",[
  "Standard room dehumidifiers struggle once the air gets dry, which is exactly when you still need water pulled out of deep material. Low grain refrigerant units keep working at lower humidity levels.",
  "That is the difference between a room that feels dry and a wall cavity that reads dry on a meter."]),
 ("How Many of Each",[
  "Counts come from the volume of the affected area, the class of water loss and how much material is wet. They are calculated, not estimated by eye.",
  ("table",["Scenario","Typical setup"],
   [["One bedroom, wet carpet only","Several air movers and one dehumidifier"],
    ["Open plan with wet drywall two feet up","More air movers plus cavity drying, one to two dehumidifiers"],
    ["Whole floor after a supply line failure","Equipment staged by room with dehumidifiers sized to total volume"]])]),
 ("What Goes Wrong Without Balance",[
  ("ul",["Too many air movers, too little dehumidification: humidity spikes and moisture migrates",
         "Dehumidifier only: surfaces dry slowly and cavities barely move",
         "Equipment switched off overnight: material reabsorbs moisture and days are added",
         "Air movers pointed straight at a wall: creates noise, not evaporation"])]),
 ("What You Should See on a Job",[
  "Air movers angled along surfaces at low angles, dehumidifiers with clear intake, the structure closed up, and a technician taking readings each day rather than assuming the plan is working."])],
"guides_h2":"More on Drying and Equipment",
"svcblurbs":["Calculated equipment loads, daily moisture logs and verified dry standards.",
 "Truck mount and portable extraction before drying equipment goes in."],
"faq_h2":"Drying Equipment Questions From Largo Owners",
"faqs":[
 ("Do I need both an air mover and a dehumidifier?","Yes. Air movers evaporate moisture out of materials and dehumidifiers remove it from the air. One without the other leaves water in the building."),
 ("Can I use household fans instead of air movers?","No. Household fans circulate room air but do not break the saturated layer against wet surfaces, so material stays damp while humidity rises."),
 ("Will a store bought dehumidifier dry my house?","It helps a room feel drier but is not sized for the volume of water a wet structure releases, and most stop working efficiently once air gets dry."),
 ("How many air movers does a room need?","It depends on room volume, how much material is wet and the class of water. Counts are calculated rather than guessed, which is why they vary."),
 ("Can I turn the equipment off at night?","No. Drying is cumulative and each shutdown lets material reabsorb moisture, which adds days and cost to the job."),
 ("Should windows be open while equipment runs?","No. In Florida, outside air at a high dew point adds moisture. Drying runs as a closed system with the dehumidifiers doing the work."),
 ("Does air conditioning replace a dehumidifier?","No. AC lowers humidity somewhat but is not built to remove the volume of water a wet structure gives off. It supports the equipment rather than replacing it."),
 ("How loud is the equipment?","Air movers produce steady noise similar to a strong fan. Most people stay in the home, and placement can usually be adjusted around bedrooms."),
 ("What does the equipment cost to run?","A typical residential setup adds a modest amount to one electricity bill, far less than the extra demolition that under drying causes."),
 ("How do I know the equipment is working?","Daily moisture readings should move toward the baseline taken from unaffected material. If readings stall, the plan needs to change.")]},

"hardwood-floor-cupping":{
"title":"Hardwood Floor Cupping: Causes and What Saves It",
"desc":"Cupped boards mean water reached the underside of your floor. Here is what cupping is, what saves a floor and when it is too late.",
"h1":"Hardwood Floor Cupping: What It Means and What Saves It",
"eyebrow":"Materials and salvage","img":"water-damage-hallway-air-movers-largo-home.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Hardwood floor cupping is the first visible sign that water reached the underside of your boards. The edges rise higher than the centres because the bottom of each board absorbed more moisture than the top, and the wood swelled unevenly.",
 "Cupping is not automatically a replacement. In a Largo home, floors caught within the first day or two are often saved. Floors left to dry on their own usually are not."],
"sections":[
 ("Cupping and Crowning Are Not the Same",[
  ("table",["","Cupping","Crowning"],
   [["Shape","Edges higher than the centre","Centre higher than the edges"],
    ["Cause","Underside wetter than the surface","Usually sanding a floor before it finished drying"],
    ["Timing","Appears within days of a water loss","Appears after a premature refinish"],
    ["Fix","Controlled drying, then refinish if needed","Dry fully, then re sand"]]),
  "The practical lesson from crowning is that sanding a floor that is still releasing moisture creates a second, avoidable problem."]),
 ("Why the Underside Gets Wet",[
  "On a slab home, water spreads sideways under the flooring. It reaches the underside of the boards through the seams and along the subfloor or slab surface, and it stays there because the finished top side seals the escape route.",
  "That is why a floor can look only slightly damp on the surface while the boards are saturated from below."]),
 ("Mat Drying: What Actually Saves a Floor",[
  "Sealed mats are placed on the floor surface and connected to a vacuum system. Air is pulled up through the boards, drawing moisture out of the assembly without lifting the floor.",
  "It is the method that makes salvage possible. Air movers blowing across the top of a cupped floor do very little, because the water is underneath."]),
 ("The Time Window",[
  ("table",["Time since the loss","Typical outcome"],
   [["Under 48 hours","Mat drying frequently saves solid hardwood"],
    ["2 to 7 days","Salvage possible but less certain, depends on species and finish"],
    ["Over a week","Replacement is likely, especially with engineered plank"]])]),
 ("Solid Versus Engineered",[
  "Solid hardwood is a single piece of wood. It swells and shrinks, and if dried under control it often returns close to flat.",
  "Engineered plank has a plywood core. Once water reaches that core the layers delaminate, and delaminated plank does not recover no matter how well it is dried."]),
 ("Why Slow Is Correct",[
  "Drying wood too aggressively causes cracking, checking and split seams. Equipment is controlled so the floor gives up moisture at a rate the wood can handle.",
  "This is why hardwood jobs run longer than carpet or drywall, often a week or more with mats running."]),
 ("When to Refinish",[
  "Not until the boards reach a dry standard matched to unaffected wood in the same house. Some residual cupping relaxes during drying, and sanding before that happens removes material you cannot put back."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Materials and Salvage Guides",
"svcblurbs":["Mat systems and controlled dehumidification to save cupped wood floors.",
 "Extraction, pad replacement and in place drying for wet carpet."],
"faq_h2":"Hardwood Water Damage Questions in Largo",
"faqs":[
 ("Can cupped hardwood flatten again?","Often yes, if controlled drying starts early. Some cupping relaxes during drying and the rest is addressed with sanding once the wood reaches a dry standard."),
 ("How fast do I need to act on a wet wood floor?","Within a day or two. Mat drying started early frequently saves solid hardwood, while a floor left a week usually becomes a replacement."),
 ("What is mat drying?","Sealed mats placed on the floor connect to a vacuum that pulls moisture up through the boards, drying the assembly from above without lifting the floor."),
 ("Can engineered hardwood be saved?","Sometimes, and less often than solid wood. Once water reaches the plywood core the layers delaminate, and delaminated plank does not recover."),
 ("Is laminate ever salvageable?","Rarely. Most laminate has a fibreboard core that swells permanently once wet, and swollen edges do not return to shape."),
 ("How long does hardwood drying take?","Commonly a week or more with mats running. Wood releases moisture slowly and rushing it with heat causes cracking and checking."),
 ("Will the floor need refinishing after drying?","Often yes, once the boards reach the dry standard. Sanding before the wood is fully dry causes crowning, which means doing the work twice."),
 ("Why not just replace the floor?","Replacement usually costs more than drying and may not match the rest of the house. Drying is attempted first when the readings support it."),
 ("Does insurance pay for drying instead of replacement?","Insurers generally prefer drying when it is viable, because it costs less. Documentation of the readings is what supports either decision."),
 ("What is crowning?","The opposite of cupping, with board centres higher than the edges. It usually means the floor was sanded before it finished drying.")]},

"flood-insurance-vs-homeowners":{
"title":"Flood Insurance or Homeowners: Which One Pays?",
"desc":"Water from outside is flood. Water from inside is homeowners. In Pinellas County that single line decides who pays for the damage.",
"h1":"Flood Insurance or Homeowners: Which One Pays",
"eyebrow":"Insurance and cost","img":"flooded-storage-room-standing-water-largo.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Flood insurance and homeowners insurance cover different water, and in Pinellas County that distinction decides a great many claims. Water that rises from outside the building is flood. Water that escapes from inside it is homeowners.",
 "The damage can look identical. A soaked living room from storm surge and a soaked living room from a burst pipe are handled by two completely different policies."],
"sections":[
 ("The Dividing Line",[
  ("table",["Source of water","Policy that responds"],
   [["Burst supply line or failed hose","Homeowners"],
    ["Water heater rupture","Homeowners"],
    ["Appliance overflow","Homeowners"],
    ["Wind driven rain through a storm damaged roof","Homeowners, under wind coverage"],
    ["Storm surge","Flood"],
    ["Street or drainage overflow entering the house","Flood"],
    ["Tidal or king tide flooding","Flood"],
    ["Groundwater rising through the slab","Flood"]])]),
 ("What Counts as a Flood",[
  "Flood policies define flooding as a general and temporary condition of partial or complete inundation of normally dry land, usually affecting two or more properties or a large area.",
  "That definition is why a burst pipe that puts an inch of water through your house is not a flood, even though it looks like one. Water has to come from outside and affect the area, not just your building."]),
 ("Two Separate Limits Inside a Flood Policy",[
  "Flood coverage splits into building and contents, and they are bought separately. Owners who carry building coverage only are sometimes surprised to find furniture and belongings are not included.",
  "Coverage below the base flood elevation is also restricted. In an elevated beach property, the ground level enclosure usually has very limited coverage for both building elements and contents."]),
 ("Where Pinellas Owners Get Caught",[
  ("ul",["Assuming homeowners covers rising water because the policy is comprehensive",
         "Carrying building flood coverage but no contents",
         "Storing valuables in a ground level enclosure below the flood elevation",
         "Buying flood coverage during hurricane season and finding a waiting period applies",
         "Not knowing their own elevation relative to base flood elevation"])]),
 ("The Waiting Period Nobody Plans For",[
  "New flood policies commonly carry a waiting period before coverage takes effect. Buying when a storm is already named is usually too late.",
  "The practical consequence is simple. Flood coverage is bought before the season, not during it."]),
 ("Why Documentation Settles the Argument",[
  "When both perils could plausibly apply, the claim turns on evidence of where the water came from. Photographs of the water line inside and outside the building, with a tape measure in frame, do more than any description.",
  "Documenting the source and the height early is what keeps a claim with the right carrier."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Insurance Guides",
"svcblurbs":["Documented drying with daily readings and photographs for your claim.",
 "Phased commercial dry out with records formatted for adjusters."],
"faq_h2":"Flood and Homeowners Coverage Questions",
"faqs":[
 ("Does homeowners insurance cover flooding in Florida?","No. Standard homeowners policies exclude water that rises from outside the building. Storm surge, drainage overflow and tidal flooding all need a flood policy."),
 ("What counts as a flood for insurance purposes?","Water that inundates normally dry land from outside, usually affecting a wider area rather than one building. A burst pipe is not a flood regardless of how much water there is."),
 ("Do I need flood insurance in Largo?","It depends on your elevation and flood zone rather than the city. Low lying and coastal parcels carry the most exposure, and lenders often require coverage in mapped zones."),
 ("Are building and contents covered together under flood?","No, they are separate limits bought separately. Owners with building coverage only often find their belongings are not included."),
 ("Is my downstairs enclosure covered?","Coverage below the base flood elevation is restricted for both building elements and contents, which matters for elevated beach properties."),
 ("Is there a waiting period on a new flood policy?","Commonly yes. Buying coverage once a storm is named is usually too late, which is why flood policies are arranged before the season."),
 ("Who decides whether it was flood or not?","The adjuster, based on evidence of where the water came from. Photographs of the water line inside and outside with a measurement are the strongest record."),
 ("Does wind driven rain count as flooding?","No. Rain entering through a wind damaged roof or window is generally handled under the wind portion of a homeowners policy."),
 ("What is an elevation certificate?","A document showing how your finished floor compares to base flood elevation. It affects your flood premium and what can be required after a major repair."),
 ("Can I claim on both policies for one event?","Sometimes, when separate causes damaged separate parts of the property. Each carrier evaluates its own peril, which makes documentation of the source critical.")]},
}
